.Dd September 30, 2026
.Dt ZPOOL-SCRUB-EXPORTER 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm zpool-scrub-exporter
.Nd write each pool's scrub state and error counts where node_exporter will serve them
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Nm
.Fl h | -help
.Sh DESCRIPTION
For every imported pool,
.Nm
reads
.Ql zpool status
and writes:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Ql zpool_scrub_last_timestamp{pool}
When the last completed scrub ended, in seconds since the epoch.
.It Ql zpool_scrub_last_duration_seconds{pool}
How long it took.
.It Ql zpool_scrub_last_errors{pool}
The errors it reported.
.It Ql zpool_scrub_in_progress{pool}
1 while a scrub runs, else 0.
.It Ql zpool_scrub_progress_ratio{pool}
From 0 to 1; only while a scrub runs.
.It Ql zpool_read_errors{pool,vdev} , Ql zpool_write_errors{pool,vdev} , Ql zpool_checksum_errors{pool,vdev}
The three error columns of every device in the pool's configuration.
.El
.Pp
The file is written beside the real one and renamed over it.
.Pa zpool-scrub-exporter.timer
runs it 15 seconds after boot and every 60 seconds after that, as root.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl h , Fl -help
Print the header comment and exit 0, writing nothing.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector/zpool-scrub.prom
What it writes.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
0.
A pool whose status cannot be read, or a line that does not parse, is
left out rather than failing the run.
.Sh EXAMPLES
On a kldload desktop on 2026-09-30, with two pools:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ grep -v '^#' /var/lib/node_exporter/textfile_collector/zpool-scrub.prom
zpool_scrub_last_duration_seconds{pool="rpool"} 20
zpool_scrub_last_timestamp{pool="rpool"} 1788098883
zpool_scrub_last_errors{pool="rpool"} 0
zpool_scrub_in_progress{pool="rpool"} 0
zpool_read_errors{pool="rpool",vdev="nvme0n1p2"} 0
zpool_write_errors{pool="rpool",vdev="nvme0n1p2"} 0
zpool_checksum_errors{pool="rpool",vdev="nvme0n1p2"} 0
zpool_scrub_last_duration_seconds{pool="tank"} 1839
zpool_scrub_last_timestamp{pool="tank"} 1785218199
zpool_scrub_last_errors{pool="tank"} 0
zpool_scrub_in_progress{pool="tank"} 0
zpool_read_errors{pool="tank",vdev="nvme-WD_BLACK_SN850X_4000GB_24405U800433"} 0
zpool_write_errors{pool="tank",vdev="nvme-WD_BLACK_SN850X_4000GB_24405U800433"} 0
zpool_checksum_errors{pool="tank",vdev="nvme-WD_BLACK_SN850X_4000GB_24405U800433"} 0
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Pp
The same numbers as node_exporter serves them:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
$ curl -s http://127.0.0.1:9100/metrics | grep -E '^zpool_scrub_(in_progress|last_duration)'
zpool_scrub_in_progress{pool="rpool"} 0
zpool_scrub_in_progress{pool="tank"} 0
zpool_scrub_last_duration_seconds{pool="rpool"} 20
zpool_scrub_last_duration_seconds{pool="tank"} 1839
# (exited 0)
.Ed
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr arcstats-exporter 8 ,
.Xr kfire-exporter 8 ,
.Xr zpool-scrub 8 ,
.Xr zpool-status 8
.Sh HISTORY
On 2026-09-30, writing this page, both found by giving it the output
.Xr zpool-status 8
prints in those cases:
a scrub that took more than a day, which
.Xr zpool 8
prints as
.Ql 1 days 02:03:04 ,
got no duration at all; and a scrub in progress past 10% was misread
from its last digit, so 12.08% was reported as 0.0208.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An kldload Aq Mt anthony@kldload.com
.Sh CAVEATS
Only scrubs are counted.
A finished resilver, a cancelled scrub, or a pool never scrubbed has
.Ql zpool_scrub_in_progress
0 and no timestamp, so an alert on the age of the last scrub fires for
it, which is usually what is wanted.
.Pp
The timestamp comes from the date
.Xr zpool 8
prints, read in the local time zone.
